  "hyperbolic_dimension": 24,
  "mirror_norm_gate_proof": "Put pi=sqrt(2), a=-3+4*pi, and p=(a), of ideal norm 23. For a primitive r=(x,y), reflection integrality is (q(r))|2I(r), with I(r)=(a*x,(y,O24)). Away from (pi) and p, any prime dividing q would divide x and, by unimodularity, y, contradicting primitivity. At (pi), a is a unit and primitivity makes I(r) a unit, so v_pi(q)<=v_pi(2)=2; oddness supplies no even-lattice lower bound. Thus the dyadic exponents are among 0,1,2. At p, a positive q-valuation and q|2(y,O24) force y in p*O24. Then x is a p-unit, I(r) has valuation one, and q=-a*x^2 mod p^2 has valuation exactly one. Hence the p-exponent is 0 or 1, and the exponent-one classes have the spatial gate y in a*O24. Modulo totally-positive unit squares the six generators are 1,2+pi,2,5+pi,12+7*pi,10+2*pi.",
